<article class="env-lemma" id="05DQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05DQ">Lemma <span data-tag="05DQ">10.14.3</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. The functors $\text{Mod}_ S \to \text{Mod}_ R$, $N \mapsto N_ R$ (restriction) and $\text{Mod}_ R \to \text{Mod}_ S$, $M \mapsto M \otimes _ R S$ (base change) are adjoint functors. In a formula </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(M, N_ R) = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ S(M \otimes _ R S, N)  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $\alpha : M \to N_ R$ is an $R$-module map, then we define $\alpha ' : M \otimes _ R S \to N$ by the rule $\alpha '(m \otimes s) = s\alpha (m)$. If $\beta : M \otimes _ R S \to N$ is an $S$-module map, we define $\beta ' : M \to N_ R$ by the rule $\beta '(m) = \beta (m \otimes 1)$. We omit the verification that these constructions are mutually inverse. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>